# ╔═╡ 3dee27c7-48a0-4c84-82d9-3e606ac14cf6
md"
---
###### 3.3 [Fermat's Little Theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermat%27s_little_theorem)

###### 3.3.1 [Fermat's Little Theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermat%27s_little_theorem) (*long* version)

*If $p$ is a prime number and $a$ is any positive integer less than $p$, then $a$ raised to the $p$th power is congruent to $a$ modulo $p$* (SICP, 1996; [2016, p.67](https://web.mit.edu/6.001/6.037/sicp.pdf)):

$\boxed{\text{If } prime(p) \text{ then } \forall a \in [1 < a < p]: a^p \equiv a\ (\bmod p)}$

Another (*short*) form can be obtained by dividing both sides of the equivalence by $a$ if $a$ is *not* divisible by $p$; that is, if $a$ is [*coprime*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coprime_integers) to $p$:

$\boxed{\text{If } prime(p) \text{ then } \forall a \in [1 < a < p] \land gcd(a,p)=1: a^{p-1} \equiv 1\ (\bmod p)}$

where $a \in [1 < a < p] \subset \mathbb N$ is called *basis* and where:

$a^p \equiv a\ (\bmod p) \text{ is an abbreviation for: } a^p\ (\bmod p) = a\ (\bmod p).$

*Two numbers are said to be congruent modulo $p$ if they both have the same remainder when divided by $p$. The remainder of a number a when divided by $p$ is also referred to as the 'remainder of a modulo p', or simply as 'a modulo p'.* (SICP)

We can summarize *Fermat's Little Theorem* with $|FermatTest \times GroundTruth| = 4$  *conditional* probabilities between the two random variables $GroundTruth = \{prime, composite\}$ and $FermatTest = \{true\  (=prime), false\ (=composite)\}$:

$\boxed{\mathbb P\left(FermatTest\ |\ GroundTruth \right).}$

"

# ╔═╡ 84499038-4ed6-46fc-a6d1-70a5964c3f3e
md"
---
###### 3.3.1 *Modular-Exponentiation* $expmod$

*To implement the Fermat test, we need a procedure that computes
the exponential of a number modulo another number* (SICP, 1996; 2016, p.67; Cormen, 2022, ch. 31.6, p.935):

$a^b \bmod n =
\begin{cases}
1,  & \text{ if } b = 0  \\
\left(a^{(b/2)}\right)^2 \;\bmod n,  & \text{ if } (b > 0) \land iseven(b)\\
b * a^{(b-1)} \bmod n,  & \text{ if } (b > 0) \land isodd(b)  \\
\end{cases}.$

"

# ╔═╡ 99f32ce2-e7c5-4a0a-b466-d7ecf5ef6af4
md"
... with *self-defined* type $FloatOrInteger.$ The same function is implemented in *Julia* under the name $powermod(base, exp, n).$
"

# ╔═╡ 8bbdd3d4-9a2b-478d-8ba4-7ca3a10aa86e
FloatOrSigned = Union{AbstractFloat, Signed}

# ╔═╡ 0af40eda-a6fd-4fb3-9d77-129c02d66b79
function expmod(base::Signed, exp::FloatOrSigned, n::Signed)
	#---------------------------------------------------------
	exp == 0    ? 1 :
	iseven(exp) ? expmod(base, exp / 2, n)^2 % n :
		   base * expmod(base, exp - 1, n) % n
	#---------------------------------------------------------
end # function expmod

