md"
---
##### 4.6 [Binary Classification](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evaluation_of_binary_classifiers) in the Domain of Primes

###### 4.6.1 Conditional Probabilities in *Causal* Direction $\mathbb P\{FermatTest\ |\ GroundTruth\}$ (e.g. *Recall*)

In a *standard* binary classifier are *four* conditional probabilities in *causal* direction. In a *Bayesian* context they are all called *likelihoods*. Two bear special names. The *first* is called *TPR (= True Positive Rate)*, *recall* in computer science and *sensivity* in medicine. The *fourth* is called *TNR (=True Negative Rate)* or *specificity* in medicine. 

$(C1)\ TPR := \mathbb P(FermatTest=true\ |\ GroundTruth=prime)\;;\text{(= recall, sensivity)}$

$(C2)\ \mathbb P(FermatTest=false\ |\ GroundTruth=prime)$

$(C1) + (C2) = 1$

$(C3)\ \mathbb P(FermatTest=true\ |\ GroundTruth=composite)$

$(C4)\ TNR := \mathbb P(FermatTest=false\ |\ GroundTruth=composite)\;;\text{(= specificity)}$

$(C3) + (C4) = 1$

Most interesting for us are (C1) and (C3):

- (C1) *TPR* (= *recall*, *sensivity*) of Fermat's test $\mathbb P(FermatTest=true\ |\ GroundTruth=prime)$ is *perfect*. So its value is $=1$.

$\boxed{(C1)\ \mathbb P(FTest=true|GT=prime) = 1\ }$

- Unfortunately probability (C3)

$\boxed{(C3)\ \mathbb P(FTest=true\ |\ GTruth=composite) ≥ 0}$

is only *zero* for *composites* which *don't lie* so are *not* *Carmichael* numbers *nor* *pseudo*primes. So we have to partition the set of *composites* into the three classes *Carmichaels*, *pseudoprimes* and *composites* which are left.

For *Carmichael* numbers this probability (C3.1) is $1$ because they *fool* the *Fermat test* *permanently*:

$\boxed{(C3.1)\ \mathbb P(FTest=true|GT=comp_{ca}) = 1\ }$

There are *composites* (*pseudo*primes) which are *not* Carmichael numbers. They *fool* the Fermat test *ocassionally* for certain bases $a$. *Number-theoretic* considerations argue that the conditional probability (C3.2) is $≤ 1/2$.

$\boxed{(C3.2)\ 0 ≤ \mathbb P(FTest=true|GT=comp_{pp}) ≤ 1/2\ }$

The rest class is for *composites* which are neither *Carmichaels* nor *pseudoprimes*. They *cannot* fool Fermat's test, so the probability (C3.3) is:

$\boxed{(C3.3)\ \mathbb P(FTest=true|GT=comp_{npp}) = 0\ }$

"

# ╔═╡ e5aa29c9-eb05-43df-a037-205cfd04dc32
md"
- (C4) *TNR* (= *specifity*) = $\mathbb P(FermatTest=false|comp)$ is *complementary* to (C3) so that

$(C3) + (C4) = (C3) + TNR = 1$

The three conditional probabilities (C4.1)-(C4.3) *of not fooling* Fermat's test are complementary to the probabilities (C3.1)-(C3.3) above:

$\boxed{1 - (C3.3) = (C4.3)\ \mathbb P(FTest=false|GT=comp_{npp}) = 1\ }$

$\boxed{1 - (C3.1) = (C4.1)\ \mathbb P(FTest=false|GT=comp_{ca}) = 0\ }$

$\boxed{1 - (C3.2) = (C4.2)\ 1/2 <\mathbb P(FTest=false|GT=comp_{pp}) ≤ 1\ }$

These considerations demonstrate that we do *not* have a *standard* binary classification problem with a $2 \times 2$ but with a $2 \times 4$-contingency table. This special table has *two* rows for $FTest=\{prime, false\}$ and *four* columns for $GTruth=\{prime, comp_{ca}, comp_{pp}, comp_{npp}\}$.
"

# ╔═╡ 374eccff-3888-4760-9ce7-09b388beaed0
md"
---
###### 4.6.2 Conditional Probabilities in *Diagnostic* Direction $\mathbb P\{GroundTruth\ |\ FermatTest\}$ (e.g. *Precision*)

In a *standard*, binary classification there are also *four* conditional probabilities in *diagnostic* direction. In a *Bayesian* context they are all called *posteriors* with [*PPV (Positive Predictive Value)*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Positive_and_negative_predictive_values) and [*NPV (Negative Predictive Value)*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Positive_and_negative_predictive_values): 

$(D1)\ PPV := \mathbb P(GroundTruth=prime\ |\ FermatTest=true)\;;\text{ precision}$

$(D2)\ \mathbb P(GroundTruth=composite\ |\ FermatTest=true)$

$(D1) + (D2) = 1$

$(D3)\ \mathbb P(GroundTruth=prime\ |\ FermatTest=false)$

$(D4)\ NPV := \mathbb P(GroundTruth=composite\ |\ FermatTest=false)$

$(D3) + (D4) = 1$

For us the most important are (D1) $PPV$ (=*precision*) and (D2) $1-PPV$.

- (D1) *PPV* (= *precision*) is between $1/2$ and $1$ depending on the range of numbers tested (here: $2 ≤ p ≤ 10^9$) and only *one* number of test *applications*. By repeating the test with *random* bases $a$ we can detect at least *pseudo*primes with a high probability.

$\boxed{(D1)\ 1/2 < PPV = \mathbb P(GT=prime|FT=true) \le 1\ }$

$\boxed{(D2)\ 0 < \mathbb P(GT=comp|FT=true) \le 1/2}$

The meaning of these conditional probabilities is that if the *FermatTest* rings *alarm* the probability that it has detected a *prime* is *higher* than for detecting a *composite*. This is a rather weak result. So a simple but crude method to overcome this deficiency is to *repeat* the test with *different random* bases $a$.

- The $NPV$ (= *Negative Predictive Value*) is perfect:

$\boxed{(D4)\ NPV = \mathbb P(GT=comp|FT=false) = 1}.$

- its complement $(D3)\ 1-NPV$ is also consequent:

$\boxed{(D3)\ \mathbb P(GT=prime|FT=false) = 0}.$

"

