###### 4.6.4 Explanantions for *JFD* Cell Entries

###### 4.6.4.1 *Frequency* of Primes $\pi(p)=N_{prime}(p)$ and the [*Prime Number Theorem*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prime_number_theorem)
(column 1 in table above)

The *prime frequency distribution* function $\pi(p)$ and its *approximation* $\frac{p}{\ln p}$ (*Prime Number Theorem*) are:

$\pi(p) = \sum_{i=1}^p \mathbf{1} \left(isprime(p)\right) \approx \frac{p}{\ln p}$

with the *indicator* function $\mathbf 1.$

As a small example for $p=10$ we have 4 primes $\pi(10) = 4$ which $2, 3, 5, 7$. For our example $n = 10^9$ we have:

$\pi(10^9) = \mathbf{50,847,534}  \approx p \cdot \frac{1}{\ln p} = \mathbf{48,254,942} \text{ (CRLS, 2022, ch. 31.8, p.943) }$

The relative deviation *error* of $\pi(10^9)$ is 

$\frac{|50847534-48254942|}{50847534} = 0.051\;\text{ or } 5.1\%.$

Because *all* primes can be detected by the *FermatTest* 

$TP = N_{prime}(p) = \mathbf{50,847,534}$ and 

$FP = N_{prime}(p) - TP = \mathbf 0.$

"

