###### 4.6.3 Derivation of *Posterior* Probabilities

There are *two* alternatives in the derivation of *posteriors*. The *first* method is of plugging in the probabilities of the JPD into the formula for *PPV* and *NPV* to get the *posterior* probabilities. The *second* method is using [*Bayes Theorem*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bayes%27_theorem). For this approach we need *priors* and *likelihoods* to derive the *posteriors*. here we prefer the first method.

"

# ╔═╡ e569b6d9-dbae-4530-b748-61b7a0ef98c5
md"
###### *PPV (= Positive Predictive Value)* and *NPV (= Negative Predictive Value)*
Both PPV and NPV are in a *Bayesian* sense *posterior* probabilites. They confirm that the FermatTest applied *only once* is a rather uncertain measurement device even though its *validity* is suspected *higher than* $0.51$.

Also we use here *complement* values which we call *PEV* (Positive *Error* Value) and *NEV* (Negative *Error* Value):

$PPV = \frac{TP}{TP+FN_{ca}+ \ll FN_{pp}+FN_{co}} ≈ 0.85$
$PEV = 1 - PPV$

$NPV = \frac{TN_{ca}+TN_{pp}+TN_{co}}{TN_{ca}+TN_{pp}+TN_{co}+FP} = 1.00$
$NEV = 1 - NPV$

When we apply the *FTest* *several* times with different assumed *independently* selected *random* bases $a$ the overall *error* probability gets multiplicatively *smaller* and as a consequence the $PPV$ complementary *greater*:

$PEV(nTrials) = (1-PPV)^{nTrials}$
$PPV(nTrials) = 1 - PEV(nTrials)$
"

# ╔═╡ 7d27a702-1d94-47cc-93f6-a0b6af4a3ee0
ppv(tp, fn_ca, fn_pp, fn_npp) = 
	tp/(tp + fn_ca + fn_pp + fn_npp)

# ╔═╡ 7fb21993-20b5-46ae-9259-6fcbe4fee6d8
ppv(tp, fn_ca, fn_pp, fn_npp)                  # ==> PPV = 0.84 --> :)

# ╔═╡ 97483911-cd38-488b-83a3-1e1d2e7f0787
npv(fp, tn_ca, tn_pp, tn_npp) = 
	(tn_ca + tn_pp + tn_npp)/(fp + tn_ca + tn_pp + tn_npp)

# ╔═╡ 0d55bd89-2604-4600-9270-da3c5a15de08
npv(fp, tn_ca, tn_pp, tn_npp)                   # ==> NPV = 1.00 --> :)

# ╔═╡ e5c42d96-697c-4c52-a83e-21435298f9d3
pev(tp, fn_ca, fn_pp, fn_npp; nTrials=1) = 
	(1 - ppv(tp, fn_ca, fn_pp, fn_npp))^nTrials # positive error value for nTrials

# ╔═╡ 9b5fd434-01b6-43ed-8306-1303ad382063
pev(tp, fn_ca, fn_pp, fn_npp; nTrials=1)        # ==> PEV = 1- PPV = 0.16 --> :)

# ╔═╡ 4504e86c-8d6f-40f3-add7-49ebd0a8de08
function ppvVal(tp, fn_ca, fn_pp, fn_npp; nTrials=1)
	nTrials == 1 ?
		ppv(tp, fn_ca, fn_pp, fn_npp) :         # positive predictive value
		1 - pev(tp, fn_ca, fn_pp, fn_npp; nTrials=nTrials)
end # function ppvVal

